#
# What OnFailure= reaches when a supervised unit fails (H-95, issue #300).
#
# This is the `RepeatedRefusal` emitter. `certimining-anchor-b-worker.service` names it as
# `certimining-alert@%n.service`, so `%i` here is the failed unit's full name — `%n` expands to the
# name including its `.service` suffix, which is why the instance is used directly and not suffixed
# again.
#
# **It asks systemd what happened rather than trusting when it was called, and that is the whole
# design of this unit.** `Thresholds::DEPLOYED` carries `consecutive_refusals: 3`, so this must page
# on the third refusal and not the first. The obvious reading of the documentation is that it does:
# `systemd.unit(5)` says OnFailure= "activates units when this unit enters the failed state", and a
# unit with Restart= set is documented to reach `failed` only once its start limit is hit.
#
# That is not reliable, and it was checked rather than assumed. systemd's own issue tracker reports
# OnFailure= firing on every auto-restart instead of once at the limit — systemd/systemd#8398 on
# v237, systemd/systemd#27594, and systemd/systemd#33710 reporting once-only on v249 and
# every-restart on v255. `RestartMode=normal`, the default, is documented as restarting "by going
# through a failed/inactive state", which is the mechanism behind those reports. So on at least some
# versions, a unit configured the obvious way pages on the first refusal while claiming to page on
# the third — a threshold of three enforced by nothing, which is the defect class this issue was
# filed under, reappearing one layer out.
#
# ExecCondition= below therefore reads `Result` from systemd and pages only on `start-limit-hit`.
# That holds whether OnFailure= fires once or on every restart, so the behaviour does not depend on
# the version. A condition that exits non-zero makes this unit skip without being considered failed,
# which is what ExecCondition= is for.
#
# **Still to be confirmed on the target host.** The machine this was written on has no systemd, so
# none of it has been loaded. #251 carries the first load: `systemd-analyze verify` on both units,
# then a deliberate three-refusal run to see how many times this unit is entered and how many pages
# it emits. Until that is recorded, treat the count as designed rather than demonstrated.

[Unit]
Description=CertiMining alert for %i
# Without this, a failure of the alert would try to alert about the alert.
OnFailure=

[Service]
Type=oneshot

# `-P Result` needs systemd 246 or newer for the shorthand; the deployment target is Ubuntu 24.04,
# which carries 255. `grep -qx` so that a Result merely containing the word does not match.
ExecCondition=/bin/sh -c 'systemctl show -P Result %i | grep -qx start-limit-hit'

# **One command and no shell.** An ExecStart that fails to parse leaves this unit never running, and
# OnFailure= reaching a unit that does nothing is precisely the failure this issue was about.
# `systemd-cat` runs the program and tags its output, putting one legible error-level entry in the
# journal under its own identifier. The refusal itself is already there — the worker prints it before
# exiting — so this does not duplicate it, it says where to look.
ExecStart=/usr/bin/systemd-cat -t certimining-alert -p err /usr/bin/printf '%%s\n' "RepeatedRefusal: %i reached its start limit and is no longer being restarted. Read its last refusal with: journalctl -u %i -n 40. See RUNBOOK-WORKER-RECOVERY.md."

# No [Install]: this is reached by the worker unit's OnFailure= and is never enabled on its own. A
# template instance has nothing to install.
